findings.body:
The loop in `extractSlotFromKeysResult()` runs `j <= keys_result->numkeys`, so it runs one extra iteration. On that iteration it reads `keys_result->keys[numkeys]`, which is past the valid entries, and writes `slot_buffer[numkeys]`, which is past the `malloc(sizeof(int) * numkeys)` allocation. Any command with two or more keys triggers this on every call, and it is reached from `extractKeysAndSlot()` on the regular command path.

With a small allocation, glibc may not crash, so this can look fine in casual testing. Under ASan, Valgrind, or a hardened build it is a heap-buffer-overflow. Because `keys[numkeys]` can be stale or garbage, the extra iteration can also call `keyHashSlot()` on an unrelated `argv` entry, which can produce a false cross-slot result.

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1111-1121"}

The buffer is also unnecessary (see the next finding). Removing it resolves the overflow: iterate `j < numkeys`, and compare slots without storing them.

::suggestion{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1113-1113"}
for (int j = 0; j < keys_result->numkeys; j++) {

`slot_buffer` is allocated with `malloc` and written to, but nothing ever reads it back. The function only compares each key's slot against `first_slot`, which needs no buffer. The allocation therefore adds one heap allocation and one free for every multi-key command, with no benefit.

There are two further problems with it:

- The return value of `malloc` is never checked. If it returns NULL, the write to `slot_buffer[j]` crashes the server.
- Redis allocates through `zmalloc`/`zfree` so memory is accounted for. In this tree `src/*.c` has about 309 `zmalloc(` call sites and about 13 raw `malloc(` call sites, so this one goes against the convention and skips the accounting.

Removing the buffer fixes all three points.

::diff{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1109-1124"}

The function can be simplified to a single loop without any allocation.

::suggestion{file="src/cluster.c" lines="1107-1124"}
int first_slot = INVALID_CLUSTER_SLOT;

for (int j = 0; j < keys_result->numkeys; j++) {
    robj *this_key = argv[keys_result->keys[j].pos];
    int this_slot = (int)keyHashSlot((char*)this_key->ptr, sdslen(this_key->ptr));

    if (first_slot == INVALID_CLUSTER_SLOT)
        first_slot = this_slot;
    else if (first_slot != this_slot)
        return CLUSTER_CROSSSLOT;
}
return first_slot;

The first branch returns when `num_keys < 0`, so by the time the `else if` is reached, `num_keys` is always `>= 0`. The `num_keys >= 0` test is always true and can be removed to make the control flow clearer.

::diff{file="src/server.c" lines="4141-4147"}

This is a small cleanup and not a bug.
